    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Santo Domingo was founded by the Spanish in 1496 on land that belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, and it became the first seat of Spanish colonial rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and the starting point of expeditions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and the Pacific.</li><li>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Francis Drake</a> captured the city in 1586 and Spanish policy emptied most of the island outside the capital, the city passed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> and its own independent governments, and it was the capital of free nations in 1821 and 1844.</li><li>It was named Ciudad Trujillo from 1936 to 1961, suffered a hurricane in 1930 and heavy fighting in 1965, and is today the capital and largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The first major slave revolt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> took place in Santo Domingo in 1521, when enslaved Africans rose on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-columbus">Diego Columbus</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantation.</p></aside>
    </section>

