    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1496</span><span class="rf-text">The Spanish found the settlement on the Ozama River as La Nueva Isabela</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1502</span><span class="rf-text">A hurricane destroys the city, and Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-de-ovando">Nicolás de Ovando</a> has it rebuilt across the river</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1521</span><span class="rf-text">Enslaved Africans rise on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-columbus">Diego Columbus</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantation in the first major slave revolt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1586</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Sir Francis Drake</a> captures the city and holds it for ransom</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1795</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> cedes the city and its colony to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and for almost three decades it changes hands several times</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1844</span><span class="rf-text">Santo Domingo becomes the capital of a free nation after independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1936</span><span class="rf-text">The city is renamed Ciudad Trujillo after the dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-trujillo">Rafael Trujillo</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1965</span><span class="rf-text">Fighting between Constitucionalistas and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> troops ends with 2,850 Dominicans and 44 American servicemen dead</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus.jpg" alt="Christopher Columbus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_Man,_Said_to_be_Christopher_Columbus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sebastiano del Piombo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> arrived in 1492, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno people</a> lived on the island, which they called Kiskeya ("mother of all lands") and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Ayiti</a> ("the land of the high mountains"). Columbus later named it Hispaniola. Its territory, which included that of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, consisted of five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>, Marién, Maguá, Maguana, Jaragua and Higüey, ruled respectively by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">caciques</a> Guacanagarix, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guarionex">Guarionex</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caonabo">Caonabo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohechio">Bohechío</a> and Cayacoa. The Spanish settled on the island in 1493.</p><p>Santo Domingo stands on the southern coast of Hispaniola, along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> Sea, at the mouth of the Ozama River, which divides it into eastern and western sections. The river flows 148 km before it reaches the sea, and the natural harbour at its mouth made the site an important port. Bartholomew Columbus founded the settlement in 1496 and named it La Nueva Isabela, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-isabela">La Isabela</a>, an earlier settlement in the north that was named for Queen Isabella I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. It was later named Santo Domingo in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-dominic">Saint Dominic</a>. In June 1502 a major hurricane destroyed it, and the new governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-de-ovando">Nicolás de Ovando</a>, had it rebuilt on a different site on the other side of the Ozama.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes.jpg" alt="Hernán Cortés" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Hern%C3%A1n_Cort%C3%A9s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Santo Domingo came to be known as the "Gateway to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>" and was the chief town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>. Expeditions that led to Ponce de León's colonisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez-de-cuellar">Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar</a>'s colonisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a>'s conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-nunez-de-balboa">Vasco Núñez de Balboa</a>'s sighting of the Pacific were all launched from it. It was the first seat of Spanish colonial rule in the New World, the Captaincy General of Santo Domingo.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-columbus">Diego Columbus</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>, arrived in 1509 and took the powers of viceroy and admiral. In 1512 Ferdinand established a Real <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> with three judges of appeal, and in 1514 Pedro Ibanez de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibarra">Ibarra</a> arrived with the Laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a>. The Alcázar de Colón was built in 1510 as the palace of the Columbus family. The city is the site of the first cathedral, castle, monastery, fortress and university of the New World, and the Universidad Autónoma de Santo Domingo, established in 1538, is the oldest university in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. The area around Calle Las Damas and the naval yard of Las Atarazanas was the first European commercial centre in the Americas, and the position on the Ozama was of great importance to the growth of trade in colonial times.</p><p>From the early 17th century, Spanish policies depopulated most of the island outside the capital. In the 18th century privateers from Santo Domingo patrolled the Caribbean and captured numerous enemy merchant ships.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-nunez-de-caceres"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-nunez-de-caceres.jpg" alt="José Núñez de Cáceres" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-nunez-de-caceres">José Núñez de Cáceres</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nunezcaceres.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown/Desconocido, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city was founded under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> and was the seat of its first colonial government in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-columbus">Diego Columbus</a> governed with the powers of viceroy and admiral from 1509, and the Real <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> of 1512 served as a court of appeal. In 1697 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ryswick">Treaty of Ryswick</a> included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s acknowledgement of French dominion over the western third of the island, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>.</p><p>From 1795 to 1822 the city changed hands several times along with the colony it headed. It was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1795, briefly captured by Haitian rebels in 1801, recovered by France in 1802, withstood a failed invasion from Haiti in 1805 and was reclaimed by Spain in 1809. In 1821 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-nunez-de-caceres">José Núñez de Cáceres</a> and the Criollo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a> overthrew Spanish rule and made Santo Domingo the capital of the Republic of Spanish Haiti, which Haiti annexed two months later. On 27 February 1844 the city became the capital of a free nation when it gained independence from Haiti under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-pablo-duarte">Juan Pablo Duarte</a>.</p><p>In 1861 Spain returned after a bargain with the dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-santana">Pedro Santana</a>, who annexed the nation back to Spanish rule. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-restoration-war">Dominican Restoration War</a> began in 1863, and in 1865 Queen Isabella II withdrew her soldiers. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> intervened in 1916, instituting a military leader, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-shepard-knapp">Harry Shepard Knapp</a>. Rafael Leónidas Trujillo governed from 1930, and from 1936 to 1961 the city was called Ciudad Trujillo; after his assassination it took back the name Santo Domingo.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/encomienda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/encomienda.jpg" alt="Encomienda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">Encomienda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francisco_Hernandez_Giron_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Trabajo propio. Reproducción de un dibujo de Felipe Guamán Poma de Ayala. (Own work. …, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Spanish colonial administration shaped the city's early life. Ferdinand's Real <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> of 1512 gave the colony a court, Diego de Medrano of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soria">Soria</a> was appointed royal treasurer, and in 1514 the Laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a> arrived. Rodrigo de Alburquerque was named repartidor de indios and soon named visitadores to enforce the laws. An estimated 400,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> living on the island were soon enslaved to work in gold mines; by 1508 their numbers had decreased to around 60,000 because of forced labour, hunger, disease and mass killings, and by 1535 only a few dozen were still alive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-de-ovando">Nicolás de Ovando</a>, governor from 1502, dealt most brutally with the Taíno, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomiendas</a> became corrupt and harsh. In 1510 the first sizable shipment of enslaved Africans, 250 Black Ladinos, arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and after the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> mill in the New World was established in 1516 the importation of slaves rose exponentially over the following two decades. Enslaved Africans worked on the sugar plantations, among them that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-columbus">Diego Columbus</a>.</p><p>The Colonial Zone preserves the original layout of the city and a large portion of its defensive wall, and early 16th century palatial houses and churches reflect the architecture of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Religious foundations were among the first of their kind in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>: the Catedral Santa María La Menor, the Dominican Convent, the ruins of the Monasterio de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>, and the Church and Convent of los Dominicos, the oldest Catholic building in continuous use in the Americas and the headquarters of the first university there. The Museo de las Casas Reales occupies the former Palace of the Governors and the building of the Royal Audiencia. After the events of 1821 to 1822, the city and the colony lost much of their Spanish-born peninsular population.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francis-drake.jpg" alt="Francis Drake" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Francis Drake</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gheeraerts_Francis_Drake_1591.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marcus Gheeraerts the Younger, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The hurricane of June 1502 destroyed the first settlement. In 1521 enslaved Africans led an uprising on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-columbus">Diego Columbus</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantation, the first major slave revolt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Many of the rebels escaped to the mountains and formed maroon communities, and the Admiral had many captured rebels hanged. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> leader Enriquillo, who had fled to the mountains, attacked the Spanish repeatedly for fourteen years, until in 1534 they gave him and his followers a city of their own. In 1586 the privateer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-drake">Sir Francis Drake</a> captured the city and held it for ransom. A report that reached the English government in May 1586 says that he took away 1,200 Englishmen, Frenchmen, Flemings and "Provincials" out of prison, besides 800 of the country people. His capture signalled the decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s dominion over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>. In 1655 an expedition sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> attacked the city, but a smaller force of local militias repulsed it, and Santo Domingo suffered only 25 dead.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-war-of-independence">Dominican War of Independence</a> the Dominicans fought several battles against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, among them the Battle of 19 March, the Battle of 30 March, Las Carreras and Beler, which thwarted the Haitian army's advance towards the city. On 24 and 25 October 1916 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Marines and Dominicans clashed in Santo Domingo, and two Marines and three Dominicans died. The hurricane San Zenón struck in 1930, causing major damage and killing up to 8,000 people.</p><p>In 1962 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-bosch">Juan Bosch</a> was elected president and was overthrown seven months later, which led to a civil war in the capital. Francisco Caamaño led the Constitucionalistas, who fought to restore democracy, and in 1965 United States troops invaded and fought heavily in the streets on 15 and 16 June. The fighting ended on 31 August 1965, with 2,850 Dominicans and 44 American servicemen dead. Hurricane Georges caused severe destruction in September 1998.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Colonial Zone, the oldest part of the city, has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1990. Its monuments include the cathedral, the Alcázar de Colón, which was restored to its present appearance in 1952, the Fortaleza Ozama, a surviving section of the Walls of Santo Domingo and the oldest military construction of European origin in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and the Panteón Nacional, a former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> building that now holds the remains of various renowned Dominicans. The Alcázar and Las Atarazanas are now museums.</p><p>The city marked the 500th anniversary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a>'s arrival in 1992 with the Columbus Lighthouse, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faro">Faro</a> a Colón. The Museum of the Dominican Man holds artefacts of the pre-Columbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> civilisation, and the Museo Prehispánico holds a major private collection of Taíno art. The Museo Memorial de la Resistencia Dominicana documents the struggle for freedom under the regimes of Trujillo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaguer">Balaguer</a>, and the Museo Duarte is dedicated to the hero of Dominican independence.</p><p>Santo Domingo is the capital and largest city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a> and the seat of its national government, and its metropolitan area, with more than four million people, is the largest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. The city proper, with just over one million people, is coterminous with the Distrito Nacional. It is the country's chief seaport, at the mouth of the Ozama.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Santo Domingo, on the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, was founded by the Spanish in 1496 on land held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> and became the first seat of Spanish colonial rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. It passed between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> before independence in 1844, and is now the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>.</em></p>

